WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:01.610 align:start position:0%
 
why was it important to focus on this  story and why was it important not to  make the movie that so many people  typically make about death row which is  the last-minute pardon I think when when  Alan and I first read David's story in  The New Yorker David gran what was so  clear to us besides that inchoate rage  that it really engendered in both of us  was that it seemed to be a catalogue of  everything that was wrong with the  criminal justice system it was junk  science  it was prosecutorial withholding of  Excalibur Tory evidence jailhouse  snitches and change for reduced  sentences most of all it was about  poverty and its effect on the inability  to therefore get proper representation  which inevitably would lead to the bad  result you know and I remember I had  read a book actually by Brian Stevenson  saying that you have a better chance to  if you are guilty if you are rich and  guilty you have a better chance than if  you were poor and innocent so so  therefore I we took on this with an  agenda but I will also say that it was  because we saw in it a human face we saw  the story of Liz Gilbert and and Todd  Willingham and I believed that that the  idea of trying to talk about what that  act of kindness and what a personal  connection can do between two people to  cause meaning in the worst of  circumstances would be a very strong  juxtaposition to the injustice that the  movie was telling Alan when you go after  David grands story in The New Yorker  what are the steps to get it from  publication in the magazine to developed  to actually produced what was that  journey like and how difficult was it  because obviously this is not standard  studio fair only nine years the  beginning was quite interesting because  I have a development fund so I read the  article and thought this is a fantastic  movie and I called CAA and they said  well there's other clients that wanted  so you're going to be down the line and  I said  tell whomever it is that wants to buy it  that I would like to do it and maybe  they know me and it was at whoever  thirty years by the grace of God and  it's like are you kidding let's just do  this together which is obviously an  incredibly amazing blessing because it  took nine years to make it and I think  thirty years of friendship kept us going  I want to ask you about what has  happened since David Graham's reporting  ended there's been a frontline special  about this case and a documentary called  incendiary the Willingham case was there  more information that came out through  the development of this project through  your own research that added to what  David Grant's amazing reporting already  at establish well certainly Barry Scheck  in the Innocence Project had accumulated  a remarkable amount after the fact but  mostly what we had we had Elizabeth  Gilbert as a source and she was  exceedingly generous with us and gave us  all of her correspondence with Todd and  all of his correspondence with her so  Laura I would I just add one quick thing  but in truth it's been a horror show  because every time we thought that the  Texas science forensics Commission  decided that it was faulty evidence and  we thought okay finally he's going to be  exonerated  a judge in Navarro County wrote a whole  opinion that he should be exonerated  shut down every single time since we  started working on the article there  have been numerous occasions when he  could have been exonerated and it has  just been swept away I hate to ask this  but isn't that in some ways make this  point of the film more relevant yeah  well I mean look though I think we have  a much greater sense of injustice right  now at this political moment than we  have ever had in the country I think we  have an unindicted co-conspirator who is  the President of the United States I  think we are aware of a disproportionate  distribution of income I think there's  so many reasons for us to actually have  some greater consciousness about  unfairness Laura I want to ask about Liz  and about me  is that something you were willing to do  reluctant to do and what you took away  from that beyond the gift of the letters  what was your impression about how she  saw herself in a story and how her view  of herself in this story evolved well I  first want to say how blessed I feel  that these amazing people to my left and  right invited me on this journey and you  know it's hard not to fall in love with  the idea of Liz but Liz refuted that she  was the most idyllic muse I think for  both myself and Ed if I may because with  that refusal to be Mother Teresa  she kept us I think very well in  constant search for honesty and and  availability to anyone who would witness  this story she said which I really loved  in our first conversation and our  conversation started and obviously she  does not travel easily and so she and I  started by phone conversations and  skyping together and her sharing all the  letters which was so amazing and  generous and one of the first things she  shared with me was she said you know I  don't know what you guys are thinking  but I just want to be clear I had seen  this you know a group of people getting  on buses to go to this rally and they  wanted to stand up to abolish the death  penalty and it was grandmothers and  teenagers and so many different kinds of  people fighting for this one cause and I  thought that was really interesting and  I learned that there was this program in  which you could write a letter to a  death row inmate and she said you know  there was that moment like well I'm in  between writing a play you know my kids  are growing up I've got a minute maybe I  can do this thing in fact  feel good because I've got 10 minutes I  want to feel like a good person and I  loved that she offered that sort of  seemingly I'm gonna Pat myself on the  back and be you know doing something  that's sort of an act of service she  said please make sure there's a feeling  of that in the movie that this isn't  this extraordinary person who did this  thing she was like I'm just like  everybody else is like huh why not okay  maybe that's nice  you know what now we've written letters  let me go visit him nobody else is  visiting but then you're your own  internal investment occurs when your  heart gets cracked open and you see the  loneliness of the place and you witness  the horror of death row and then  ultimately as it started to unfold she  started to have a feeling that there was  more to this story and you very  intentionally I would suspect follow the  pattern of David Grant's article and  that is if you read David's story for  the first half of it you think he's  guilty that you as the director put the  audience in the eyes of the jury and I  wonder why that was important to you  because it's important to David as a  journalist it's also important to you as  a director it is because I felt that  that what happened to Todd had to do  with an objectification or a  demonization that is also very common he  is the other and he's the thing that  society wants to blame and get rid of  and I think for us to form that same  judgment is to make us all we the  audience complicit and therefore I think  it's only because of that complicity can  you then take all the ride must you take  all the ride and be forced to see the  result Alan I'm going to ask you about  making this movie because one of the  last credits we saw was shot in Georgia  that's a conversation we can have later  but did you try to shoot it somewhere  else and never enjoyed you did you try  to go to Texas and was that was that a  possibility and if not why not  interestingly when we were first  mounting the production of someone that  I knew from some time before we'd made a  film together  was then had become the head of the  Texas Film Commission so I sent her the  screenplay and we actually really like  each other she rang me up and she said  you can't come here it's not safe and I  said these are words that are never  spoken by a phone Commissioner you can't  come here what was the concern I have to  say it's not safe well this is a very  provocative story obviously in Texas and  there's been a huge cover-up so there's  a lot invested in keeping it covered up  Allen Laura Ned thanks so much for  bringing us your film

